WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.163
Injection happens when untrusted input is interpreted as instructions or structure in the wrong context.

2
00:00:07.163 --> 00:00:21.090
JavaScript applications face this risk in many places because they interact with databases, document stores, shells, template engines, browsers, parsers, URLs, headers, file paths, and downstream APIs.

3
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:28.253
The variety of contexts is what makes injection prevention a design discipline rather than a single trick.

4
00:00:28.253 --> 00:00:31.515
The most important habit is data flow awareness.

5
00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:39.156
Know where untrusted data enters, how it is transformed, where it is stored, and which sensitive sinks it reaches.

6
00:00:39.156 --> 00:00:49.502
Client-side validation can improve usability, but server-side validation, safe construction, and context-aware output handling are still required.

7
00:00:49.502 --> 00:00:53.401
A value that is safe in one context may be unsafe in another.

8
00:00:53.401 --> 00:00:56.585
Strong prevention combines several controls.

9
00:00:56.585 --> 00:00:58.972
Use parameterized queries for SQL.

10
00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:03.588
Validate object shape and allowlisted fields for document queries.

11
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:06.055
Avoid string-built shell commands.

12
00:01:06.055 --> 00:01:10.432
Keep user content separate from templates and executable logic.

13
00:01:10.432 --> 00:01:15.048
Use safe DOM APIs and context-aware encoding for browser rendering.

14
00:01:15.048 --> 00:01:20.142
Apply least privilege so one mistake does not become a system-wide failure.

15
00:01:20.142 --> 00:01:23.245
Testing and review keep these controls alive.

16
00:01:23.245 --> 00:01:34.148
Security-focused unit and integration tests, schema tests, negative tests, dependency review, static analysis, and code review all help identify risky paths.

17
00:01:34.148 --> 00:01:40.993
Documentation helps future developers understand why a particular sink needs a particular control.

18
00:01:40.993 --> 00:01:43.460
The goal is not memorizing payloads.

19
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:50.225
The goal is designing JavaScript applications where untrusted data cannot change command meaning.

20
00:01:50.225 --> 00:01:59.775
When developers keep data as data, choose safe APIs, validate intent, and encode for the correct context, injection risk drops dramatically.
